  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,\u2713$",
